{"question": "Physical sign of severe aortic stenosis is:", "options": {"A": "A late peaking holosystolic murmur", "B": "A delayed carotid upstroke", "C": "A diastolic rumble", "D": "An opening snap"}, "pred_answer": "B", "gold_answer": "B", "question_domains": ["Cardiology", "Internal Medicine", "Cardiovascular Disease", "Physical Diagnosis", "Clinical Medicine"], "option_domains": ["Cardiology", "Physical Examination Signs"], "question_analyses": {"Cardiology": "The physical sign of severe aortic stenosis that is most commonly noted is a harsh, crescendo-decrescendo systolic murmur heard best at the right second intercostal space along the right sternal border, which radiates to the carotids. This murmur is due to the turbulent blood flow through the narrowed aortic valve. Additionally, there are other physical signs that can be indicative of severe aortic stenosis:\n\n1. **Carotid Upstroke**: The carotid pulse may be diminished or delayed, reflecting the reduced forward flow of blood from the left ventricle to the aorta. 2. **Loud S2**: The second heart sound (S2) may be loud due to the increased pressure in the left ventricle. 3. **Loud P2**: In some cases, the pulmonary component of the second heart sound (P2) may be loud due to pulmonary hypertension. 4. **Apex Murmur**: A fourth heart sound (S4) may be heard, indicating left ventricular hypertrophy and diastolic dysfunction. 5. **Gallbladder Tenderness (Traube's Sign)**: This is a less common sign where there is tenderness over the gallbladder area due to the dilated left ventricle pushing on the diaphragm. 6..", "Internal Medicine": "The physical sign of severe aortic stenosis that is most commonly noted is a harsh, crescendo-decrescendo systolic murmur heard best at the right second intercostal space along the right sternal border, which radiates to the carotids. This murmur is due to the turbulent blood flow through the narrowed aortic valve. Additionally, there are other physical signs that can be indicat